<institution>Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, Northwestern University, Feinberg School of Medicine</institution>, <addr-line>Chicago, IL</addr-line>, <country country="US">United States</country></aff><xref rid="fn-0002" ref-type="author-notes"/><xref rid="c1" ref-type="corresp"/><!--shanauer@northwestern.edu--></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><fn id="fn-0001"><p>Joint first authors.</p></fn><fn id="fn-0002"><p>Joint corresponding authors.</p></fn><corresp id="c1">Corresponding authors: Stephen B. Hanauer, Department of Medicine, Northwestern University, Feinberg School of Medicine, 676&#x000a0;N St Clair&#x02014;Suite 1400, Chicago, IL 60611, United States (<email>shanauer@northwestern.edu</email>)</corresp><corresp id="c2">Corresponding authors: Dr Bruce E. Sands, Dr. Henry D. Janowitz Division of Gastroenterology, Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, One Gustave L. Levy Place, Box 1069, New York, NY 10029, United States (<email>bruce.sands@mssm.edu</email>).</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>6</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub" iso-8601-date="2025-04-17"><day>17</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>17</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>19</volume><issue>6</issue><elocation-id>jjaf060</elocation-id><history><date date-type="corrected-typeset"><day>04</day><month>6</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of European Crohn&#x02019;s and Colitis Organisation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link>), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. For commercial re-use, please contact reprints@oup.com for reprints and translation rights for reprints. All other permissions can be obtained through our RightsLink service via the Permissions link on the article page on our site&#x02014;for further information please contact journals.permissions@oup.com.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="jjaf060.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><sec id="s1"><title>Background and Aims</title><p>In the LIBERTY phase 3 studies in Crohn&#x02019;s disease (CD) or ulcerative colitis (UC), maintenance CT-P13 subcutaneous (SC) 120&#x000a0;mg was more effective than placebo after 1 year. Here we report 2-year data from the LIBERTY open-label extensions.</p></sec><sec id="s2"><title>Methods</title><p>Two randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blind studies evaluated the efficacy and safety of CT-P13 SC maintenance in moderate-to-severe CD or UC. Responders to CT-P13 intravenous induction were randomized at week (W) 10 to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo biweekly, until W54. From W22, dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg was permitted for loss of response. At W56, patients could enter an open-label extension, receiving CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg (or 240&#x000a0;mg if dose-adjusted), biweekly, until W102.</p></sec><sec id="s3"><title>Results</title><p>The extension comprised 278/343 (81.0%) and 348/438 (79.5%) patients in the CD and UC studies, respectively. In those continuing on-study, efficacy (non-responder imputation) was well maintained in the CT-P13 SC group at W102: 63.5% (as-observed: 70.5%) and 49.0% (as-observed: 58.8%) of CD patients (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;192) achieved clinical remission and endoscopic response, respectively; 45.1% (as-observed: 60.1%) and 41.4% (as-observed: 52.4%) of UC patients (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;237) achieved clinical remission and endoscopic-histologic mucosal improvement, respectively. No new safety signals were identified from longer-term CT-P13 SC treatment or starting CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg after placebo, with similar adverse event rates for patients undergoing dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg from CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo.</p></sec><sec id="s4"><title>Conclusion</title><p>CT-P13 SC is an effective and well-tolerated long-term maintenance treatment in moderate-to-severe CD and UC.</p></sec><sec id="s5"><title>ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers</title><p>
<ext-link xlink:href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT03945019" ext-link-type="uri">NCT03945019</ext-link> (CD) and <ext-link xlink:href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT04205643" ext-link-type="uri">NCT04205643</ext-link> (UC).</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical"><title>Graphical Abstract</title><p>
<fig position="float" id="ga1"><label>Graphical Abstract</label><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig6" position="float"/></fig>
</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>CT-P13 SC</kwd><kwd>inflammatory bowel disease</kwd><kwd>LIBERTY</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group award-type="grant"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>Celltrion, Inc</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group></funding-group><counts><page-count count="15"/></counts></article-meta></front><body><sec id="s6"><title>1. Introduction</title><p>Crohn&#x02019;s disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC) are chronic, inflammatory, immunologically mediated conditions, impacting the gastrointestinal system.<sup><xref rid="CIT0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup> Tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF&#x003b1;) contributes to the pathogenesis of CD and UC by mediating pro-inflammatory effects, via numerous pathways, and is a key therapeutic target in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).<sup><xref rid="CIT0002" ref-type="bibr">2&#x02013;5</xref></sup></p><p>Following its approval for treatment of IBD, infliximab, a TNF inhibitor (TNFi), has remained a widely used and preferred treatment option for more than 20 years.<sup><xref rid="CIT0006" ref-type="bibr">6&#x02013;8</xref></sup></p><p>Intravenous (IV) CT-P13 was the first infliximab biosimilar to be approved by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) in 2013<sup><xref rid="CIT0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup> and licensed by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 2016.<sup><xref rid="CIT0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> CT-P13 IV is approved for a range of autoimmune diseases, including the treatment of moderate-to-severe CD and UC, where conventional therapies have proven ineffective or unsafe.<sup><xref rid="CIT0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref></sup> A subcutaneous (SC) formulation of CT-P13 (CT-P13 SC) has been developed, which may have several benefits over IV administration. IV anti-TNF therapies are associated with substantial non-drug costs due to their time-consuming mode of delivery.<sup><xref rid="CIT0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>,<xref rid="CIT0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup> An SC route of administration for CT-P13 may alleviate healthcare budget and resource constraints, and increased patient convenience might improve treatment adherence.<sup><xref rid="CIT0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> A completed phase 1 study in IBD demonstrated a non-inferior pharmacokinetic (PK) profile of CT-P13 SC over CT-P13 IV,<sup><xref rid="CIT0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> which resulted in the EMA expanding approval of CT-P13 SC in 2020 to include CD, UC, and other autoimmune diseases.<sup><xref rid="CIT0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>,<xref rid="CIT0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref></sup> Because of differences in administration route and PK profile between the IV and SC formulations of CT-P13, CT-P13 SC was assessed via the FDA new drug pathway, with its associated requisite for additional clinical trial evidence.<sup><xref rid="CIT0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref></sup> CT-P13 SC was approved by the FDA in 2023 for the maintenance treatment of moderately to severely active CD and UC following induction treatment with IV infliximab.<sup><xref rid="CIT0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref></sup></p><p>Two randomized, placebo-controlled phase 3 trials have evaluated the efficacy and safety of CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg maintenance treatment, following CT-P13 IV induction, in patients with moderate-to-severe CD or UC.<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> These studies showed over 1 year that maintenance therapy with CT-P13 SC was more effective than placebo in patients with CD (week [W] 54 clinical remission rates, based on Crohn&#x02019;s Disease Activity Index [CDAI] score, 62.3% vs 32.1%: <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;.0001; endoscopic response rate, 51.1% vs 17.9%: <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;.0001) and in patients with UC (W54 clinical remission rates, based on modified Mayo score, 43.2% vs 20.8%: <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;.0001).<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> Open-label extension phases of these trials evaluated the durability of treatment efficacy over a longer treatment period, along with PK, biomarkers, and safety parameters, which are reported here.</p></sec><sec id="s7"><title>2. Methods</title><sec id="s8"><title>2.1. Study design</title><p>Two randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blind phase 3 studies (LIBERTY-CD and LIBERTY-UC) were conducted to evaluate efficacy and safety of CT-P13 SC as maintenance therapy in patients with moderate-to-severe CD (NCT03945019) and UC (NCT04205643). Patients were enrolled at 114 sites in 26 countries, in the CD study, and at 92 sites in 14 countries, in the UC study.</p><p>The studies consisted of an open-label induction phase with CT-P13 IV (W0&#x02013;6), a double-blind maintenance phase in responders at W10, where patients were randomized to receive either CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo (W10&#x02013;54), and an open-label extension phase in which all patients received CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg (W56&#x02013;102) (<xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref>). Patients with no safety concerns, and whom it was considered may benefit from further treatment in the investigator&#x02019;s opinion, were entered into the extension phase. Dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg was permitted from W22 in case of loss of response, with patients to continue this dose for the remainder of the study. Full study procedures and details of study centers have been reported previously.<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup></p><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1.</label><caption><p>Study design overview. 1. CD: CDAI-100 responder, defined as a decrease in CDAI score of 100 points or more from baseline. UC: Clinical response by modified Mayo score: a decrease in modified Mayo score from baseline of&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;2 points and&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;30%, with an accompanying decrease in the rectal bleeding subscore of&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;1 point or an absolute rectal bleeding subscore of 0 or 1 point. 2. From W22 through W102, dose adjustment was allowed. Patients who received CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg could increase the dose to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, and patients who received placebo SC could receive CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, if they initially responded but then lost response according to the loss of response criteria. The adjusted dose was maintained for the remainder of the study (up to W102). 3. In the extension phase, all patients who completed the maintenance phase up to W54 and could benefit from continued treatment, in the opinion of the investigator, received active treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg via PFS (UC), or via PFS or AI (CD) from W56. AI, auto-injector; CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; CDAI, Crohn&#x02019;s Disease Activity Index; IV, intravenous; PFS, pre-filled syringe; UC, ulcerative colitis; SC, subcutaneous; W, week.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig1" position="float"><alt-text>Study design.</alt-text></graphic></fig><p>Studies were conducted according to all relevant legal and regulatory requirements, the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki,<sup><xref rid="CIT0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref></sup> and International Council for Harmonisation Good Clinical Practice guidelines.<sup><xref rid="CIT0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref></sup> Study protocols and written materials were approved by the institutional review board/institutional ethics committee of each study center. All patients provided written informed consent.</p></sec><sec id="s9"><title>2.2. Patients</title><p>Key inclusion criteria were male and female adults, aged 18&#x02013;75, with moderately to severely active CD (defined as a CDAI score &#x02265; 220 and &#x02264; 450; a Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn&#x02019;s Disease [SES-CD] of &#x02265; 6 points for ileal-colonic CD or &#x02265; 4 points including ulcer score from at least one segment for ileal CD or colonic CD; and a 7&#x02011;day average daily stool frequency [SF] of &#x02265; 4 points [of type 6 or type 7 on the Bristol Stool Form Scale (BSFS)] and/or 7&#x02011;day average worst daily abdominal pain [AP] of &#x02265; 2 points [using a 4&#x02011;point scale]), or UC (defined as a modified Mayo score &#x02265; 5 and &#x02264; 9, with an endoscopic subscore of &#x02265; 2) at screening. Eligible patients were non-responsive, intolerant, or had a contraindication to corticosteroids and/or immunomodulators (CD and UC).</p><p>Key exclusion criteria were previous treatment with &#x02265; 2 biological agents and/or JAK inhibitors, prior treatment with a TNF&#x003b1; inhibitor or biological agent within 5 half-lives prior to first administration of the study drug (day 0), previous inadequate response or intolerance to TNF&#x003b1; inhibitors, and previous treatment with infliximab. Full eligibility criteria and permitted concomitant medication use are detailed in the main studies&#x02019; publication.<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup></p></sec><sec id="s10"><title>2.3. Treatment</title><p>Following treatment with CT-P13 IV 5&#x000a0;mg/kg (W0&#x02013;6), patients who responded to treatment were randomized (2:1) at W10 to receive CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo biweekly. Patients who completed maintenance phase treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo, from W10 to W54 with no safety concerns, and who may have benefited from further treatment in the investigator&#x02019;s opinion, were entered into the extension phase. From W56 to W102, all patients received CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg biweekly via pre-filled syringe (PFS) or, in the CD study, auto-injector (AI), regardless of W10 randomization.</p><p>From W22 to W102, dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg (from CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or placebo) was permitted if patients underwent loss of response, defined as a CDAI score &#x02265; 220, with score increase of &#x02265; 100 from W10 (CD) or an increase in modified Mayo score of &#x02265; 2 and &#x02265; 30% from W10, with an actual value of &#x02265; 5 points, and an endoscopic subscore of &#x02265; 2 points (UC). Patients who underwent dose adjustment during the maintenance phase continued to receive CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in the extension phase. Prohibited therapy and permitted concomitant medications are detailed in the <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Methods</xref>.</p></sec><sec id="s11"><title>2.4. Outcomes and assessments</title><p>(Co-)primary and key secondary endpoints, as determined during maintenance phase visits, have been reported in the W54 manuscript.<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> Study visits for the extension phase were subsequently scheduled every 8 weeks from W62 to W102, with a W56 visit included only in the CD study. End-of-study (EOS) visits took place 4 weeks after administration of the final dose.</p><p>In the CD study, efficacy endpoints for the extension phase were clinical remission (CDAI score&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;150), CDAI-100 response (&#x02265;100-point decrease in CDAI score), endoscopic remission (SES-CD score &#x02264; 4 [with a &#x02265; 2-point decrease from baseline] with all subscores &#x02264; 1), endoscopic response (50% decrease from baseline in SES-CD score), clinical remission by AP and SF (average worst daily AP score of&#x02005;&#x02264;&#x02005;1 on a 4-point scale and an average daily loose/watery SF score of&#x02005;&#x02264;&#x02005;3 [of type 6 or 7 on the BSFS, with no worsening in either average score compared with the baseline value]), corticosteroid-free remission (CDAI score&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;150 without receiving any corticosteroids for &#x02265; 8 weeks prior to W102 [evaluated among patients who were using oral corticosteroids at baseline]), and deep remission (achievement of both clinical and endoscopic remission criteria). In the extension phase, CDAI, and AP and SF scores, were assessed at all scheduled visits except W56. Short Inflammatory Bowel Disease Questionnaire (SIBDQ) was assessed at W70, W86, and W102. Colonoscopy for SES-CD score was performed at W102.</p><p>Additional endpoints included mean CDAI and SIBDQ over time, and CDAI, SIBDQ, and SES-CD before dose adjustment and at W102. Durable remission (clinical remission at both W10 and W102, or at W54 and W102), change from baseline in SES-CD, CDAI score, and SIBDQ were assessed.</p><p>In the UC study, efficacy endpoints for the extension phase were clinical remission according to modified Mayo score (MMS; SF and endoscopic subscores of 0 or 1, rectal bleeding subscore of 0), clinical response (&#x02265; 2-point and &#x02265; 30% decrease from baseline in MMS [alongside &#x02265; 1-point decrease, or absolute score of 0 or 1, in rectal bleeding subscore]), endoscopic-histologic mucosal improvement (absolute endoscopic subscore 0 or 1 [MMS] and absolute Robarts Histopathology Index [RHI] score of &#x02264; 3 points [alongside lamina propria neutrophils and neutrophils in epithelium subscore of 0]), and corticosteroid-free remission (clinical remission based on MMS without receiving any corticosteroids for &#x02265; 8 weeks prior to W102). In the extension phase, MMS and RHI scores were assessed at W102 only; partial Mayo score (PMS) was assessed at all scheduled visits; and SIBDQ was assessed at W70, W86, and W102.</p><p>Additional endpoints included mean PMS and SIBDQ over time, and MMS, PMS, and SIBDQ before dose adjustment and at W102. Durable remission (clinical remission at both W10 and W102, or at W54 and W102), and change from baseline in MMS, PMS, and SIBDQ were also assessed.</p><p>Safety assessments included monitoring throughout of treatment-emergent adverse events ([TEAEs] coded using Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities v25.0 and graded according to the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events v5.0). Adverse events of special interest (AESIs) were systemic injection reaction (SIR), localized injection-site reaction (ISR), delayed hypersensitivity, infection, and malignancy. Whether patients underwent CD- or UC-related surgery was also evaluated. Immunogenicity, measured as anti-CT-P13 antibodies, was assessed at all scheduled visits using a high-sensitivity, validated, drug-tolerant immunoassay (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Methods</xref>). Treatment-emergent adverse events were recorded based on the initial occurrence date of each AE. Ongoing AEs at the start of the extension phase were not counted in the extension phase. In the extension phase, blood samples for PK analysis (infliximab trough concentration [<italic toggle="yes">C</italic><sub>trough</sub>]) were collected pre-dose at scheduled visits. Biomarker analysis of C-reactive protein (CRP) was conducted on blood samples collected for safety assessments at scheduled visits along with analysis of stool samples for fecal calprotectin (FC) at W70, W86, and W102.</p></sec><sec id="s12"><title>2.5. Statistical analysis</title><p>Analysis populations are detailed in the <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Methods</xref>.</p><p>Drug persistency in the CT-P13 SC group was analyzed in the all-randomized population using Kaplan&#x02013;Meier survival curves regardless of dose adjustment.</p><p>Efficacy endpoints were analyzed in the all-randomized population for patients who were treated in the extension phase according to randomized treatment at W10 (&#x0201c;CT-P13 SC group&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;placebo group&#x0201d;).</p><p>For efficacy outcomes relating to remission and response, or endoscopic-histologic mucosal improvement (UC study), both non-responder imputation (NRI) and as-observed analyses were performed. In NRI analyses, patients who had missing or incomplete data at their scheduled visit of interest were imputed as non-responders/non-remitters. As-observed analyses included only patients who had available data at the visit of interest, with no imputation for missing or incomplete data. Patients who underwent dose adjustment were imputed as non-responders/non-remitters for all subsequent visits up to W54 and W102 in both the NRI and as-observed analyses. Analyses were also performed regardless of dose adjustment (ie, dose-adjusted patients were not considered non-responders/non-remitters), and for those who had their dose adjusted, using NRI for patients with missing/incomplete data. In patients who underwent dose adjustment, a paired t-test was used to compare changes in SES-CD/CDAI/SIBDQ (CD) and MMS/PMS/SIBDQ (UC) from the dose adjustment visit to W102, and the proportion of patients achieving efficacy outcomes was also assessed at W102.</p><p>Safety analyses were conducted in the safety population for patients who were treated in the extension phase. In the maintenance phase, safety data are included for patients in the CT-P13 SC group regardless of dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, but only prior to dose adjustment for patients in the placebo group. In the extension phase, safety data are included for both groups (CT-P13 SC and placebo) regardless of dose adjustment. Exposure-adjusted incidence of TEAEs was assessed in the subgroups of patients who did and did not undergo dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240 mg: &#x0201c;<bold>CT-P13 SC 120 mg</bold>&#x0201d; comprises patients randomized at W10 to receive CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg during the maintenance phase and who continued to the extension phase (regardless of dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in either phase), and includes the data prior to any dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, AND patients who were randomized at W10 to receive placebo and were then switched to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg in the extension phase (and includes the data for these patients prior to any dose adjustment to CT-P13 240 SC mg); &#x0201c;<bold>CT-P13 SC 240 mg</bold>&#x0201d; comprises all patients with dose adjustment after W22, irrespective of whether the patient was randomized at W10 to receive placebo or CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg; &#x0201c;<bold>Placebo</bold>&#x0201d; comprises all patients randomized at W10 to receive placebo during the maintenance phase, then switched to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg upon entering the extension phase, or who underwent dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in the maintenance phase, and includes the data prior to switching to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg.</p><p>Immunogenicity data were also collated for the safety population. When evaluating immunogenicity for CT-P13 SC, data were considered regardless of dose adjustment at both W54 and W102. For placebo, antibody conversion prior to dose adjustment was counted at the W54 summary, while the W102 summary evaluated data regardless of dose adjustment.</p><p>Pharmacokinetic and biomarkers were analyzed in the PK and biomarker populations, respectively, for patients who were treated in the extension phase. For patients who had undergone dose adjustment, only data collected prior to dose adjustment were included in PK and biomarker analyses. For PK analysis, all concentrations below the lower limit of quantification after study-drug exposure were set to lower limit of quantification.</p><p>All analyses were conducted using Statistical Analysis System (SAS) software (SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC, USA) v9.40.</p></sec></sec><sec id="s13"><title>3. Results</title><p>Studies were carried out between October 2019 and August 2023 (CD), and September 2020 and July 2023 (UC). A summary of patient disposition in both studies, in the all-randomized population, is presented in <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>; 278/343 (81.0%) patients in the CD study (CT-P13 SC: 192/231 [83.1%], placebo: 86/112 [76.8%]) and 348/438 (79.5%) patients in the UC study (CT-P13 SC: 237/294 [80.6%], placebo: 111/144 [77.1%]) were treated in the extension phase. Overall, 241/343 (70.3%) patients completed the CD study (CT-P13 SC: 166/231 [71.9%], placebo: 75/112 [67.0%]) and 303/438 (69.2%) patients completed the UC study (CT-P13 SC: 208/294 [70.7%], placebo: 95/144 [66%]) (<xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>). For both CD and UC studies, withdrawal by subject was the most common reason for discontinuation during the extension phase.</p><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2.</label><caption><p>Summary of patient disposition in the CD study (A) and the UC study (B). <sup>1</sup>One patient from the CD study in the CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg group was excluded from all analysis populations due to significant good clinical practice non-compliance of the study center. CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; SC, subcutaneous; UC, ulcerative colitis; W, week.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig2" position="float"><alt-text>Patient disposition.</alt-text></graphic></fig><p>During the maintenance phase, in the all-randomized population, rates of dose adjustment in the CT-P13 SC and placebo groups, respectively, were 19.0% (44/231) and 43.8% (49/112) in the CD study, and 31.0% (91/294) and 52.8% (76/144) in the UC study. Dose adjustment rates during the extension phase were 18.2% (42/231) and 39.3% (44/112) in the CD study, and 24.1% (71/294) and 41.7% (60/144) in the UC study.</p><p>Baseline characteristics of patients entering the extension phase were well balanced between groups and similar to those of the all-randomized population (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S1</xref>).<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup></p><sec id="s14"><title>3.1. Efficacy</title><p>The proportion of patients in the CT-P13 SC group in the CD and UC studies who achieved efficacy endpoints at W54 and W102 are shown in <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figures 3</xref> and <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>.</p><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3.</label><caption><p>Proportion of patients in the CD study who achieved efficacy endpoints at W54 and W102 in the CT-P13 SC group (all-randomized population treated in the extension phase), showing NRI (A) and as-observed (B) analyses, as well as analyses regardless of dose adjustment (C). (A) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase; patients with missing data or those who underwent dose adjustment were considered as non-responders/non-remitters for all subsequent visits up to W54 and W102. (B) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase and had data available for the specified endpoint; patients with dose adjustment were considered non-responders/non-remitters for all subsequent visits up to W54 and W102. (C) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase; remitters/responders were determined based on whether observed data met remission/response criteria regardless of dose adjustment and patients with missing data were considered non-responders/non-remitters. Clinical remission: CDAI score&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;150; CDAI-100 response: decrease of&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;100 points from baseline in CDAI score; endoscopic remission: SES-CD score of&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;4 (&#x02265;&#x02005;2 point decrease from baseline [all subscores&#x02005;&#x02264;&#x02005;1]); endoscopic response: 50% decrease in SES-CD score from baseline; clinical remission based on AP and SF: average worst daily AP score of&#x02005;&#x02264;&#x02005;1 (using 4-point scale) and an average daily loose/watery SF score of&#x02005;&#x02264;&#x02005;3 (of type 6 or type 7 on BSFS) with no worsening in either average score compared with the baseline value; corticosteroid-free remission: clinical remission (CDAI score&#x02005;&#x0003c;&#x02005;150) and not receiving corticosteroids for&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;8 weeks prior to scheduled visit of interest among patients who were using corticosteroids at baseline; deep remission: meeting both clinical and endoscopic remission criteria. AP, abdominal pain; BSFS, Bristol Stool Form Scale; CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; CDAI, Crohn&#x02019;s Disease Activity Index; NRI, non-responder imputation; SES-CD, Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn&#x02019;s Disease; SC, subcutaneous; SF, stool frequency; W, week.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig3" position="float"><alt-text>Efficacy outcomes: CD study.</alt-text></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4.</label><caption><p>Proportion of patients in the UC study who achieved efficacy endpoints at W54 and W102 in the CT-P13 SC group (all-randomized population treated in the extension phase), showing NRI (A) and as-observed (B) analyses, as well as analyses regardless of dose adjustment (C). (A) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase; patients with missing data or those who underwent dose adjustment were considered non-responders/non-remitters for all subsequent visits up to W54 and W102. (B) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase and had data available for the specified endpoint; patients with dose adjustment were considered non-responders/non-remitters for all subsequent visits up to W54 and W102. (C) Denominator&#x02005;=&#x02005;number of patients who were treated in the extension phase; remitters/responders are determined based on whether observed data met remission/response criteria regardless of dose adjustment and patients with missing data were considered non-responders/non-remitters. Clinical remission: based on modified Mayo score (stool frequency and endoscopic subscores of 0 or 1, rectal bleeding subscore of 0); clinical response: decrease of &#x02265; 2 points and &#x02265; 30% from baseline in modified Mayo score (with rectal bleeding subscore of 0 or 1 or decrease of &#x02265; 1 point); endoscopic-histologic mucosal improvement: endoscopic subscore of 0 or 1 (modified Mayo score) and RHI score of &#x02264; 3 points (with lamina propria neutrophils and neutrophils in epithelium subscore of 0); corticosteroid-free remission: clinical remission (based on modified Mayo score) and not receiving corticosteroids for &#x02265; 8 weeks prior to scheduled visit of interest among patients who were using corticosteroids at baseline. NRI, non-responder imputation; RHI, Roberts Histopathology Index; UC, ulcerative colitis; W, week.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig4" position="float"><alt-text>Efficacy outcomes: UC study.</alt-text></graphic></fig><p>For the CD study, efficacy endpoints at W102 (NRI analyses) based on CDAI score were achieved by 122 (63.5%; clinical remission) and 130 (67.7%; CDAI-100 response) patients. Endoscopic remission and response based on SES-CD score (centrally evaluated) were achieved by 63 (32.8%) and 94 (49.0%) patients, respectively. Clinical remission based on AP and SF, and deep remission, were achieved by 112 (58.3%) and 56 (29.2%) patients, respectively. For those using corticosteroids at baseline, 33/79 patients (41.8%) were in corticosteroid-free remission at W102. The proportions of patients achieving efficacy endpoints by the as-observed analyses were slightly higher than the NRI analyses but generally similar (<xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3</xref>).</p><p>In the UC study, efficacy endpoints at W102 (NRI analyses) based on MMS were achieved by 107 (45.1%; clinical remission) and 124 (52.3%; clinical response) patients. Endoscopic-histologic mucosal improvement at W102 was achieved by 98 patients (41.4%). For those using corticosteroids at baseline, corticosteroid-free remission at W102 was achieved by 35/96 patients (36.5%). The proportions of patients achieving efficacy endpoints were slightly higher in the as-observed analysis than NRI, but generally similar (<xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4</xref>).</p><p>For both NRI and as-observed analyses, outcomes at W102 showed that efficacy at W54 was generally maintained during the extension phase for CD (<xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3</xref>) and UC (<xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4</xref>). Rates of clinical remission and response evaluated at each visit by CDAI in the CD study and by PMS in the UC study also showed maintenance of efficacy between W54 and W102 (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Figure S1</xref>).</p><p>Analyses regardless of dose adjustment (with NRI for missing/incomplete data) showed slightly higher rates of efficacy outcomes compared with the NRI analysis, in both CD (<xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3</xref>) and UC (<xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4</xref>) at W54 and W102.</p><p>Additional efficacy analyses in the CT-P13 SC groups show durable remission in 98/149 (65.8%) of CD patients from W10 to W102, and in 114/141 (80.9%) of CD patients from W54 to W102. In UC patients, durable remission was seen in 76/125 (60.8%) from W10 to W102, and in 86/125 (68.8%) from W54 to W102 (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S2</xref>). As-observed analyses support the NRI findings, with similar (CD study) or slightly higher (UC study) reported rates of durable remission from W10 to W54, and W54 to W102 compared with the NRI analysis.</p><p>For CD patients, the mean (SD) change from baseline for SES-CD, CDAI, and SIBDQ, respectively, was&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;8.5 (7.21),&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;229.64 (83.935), and 18.8 (13.83) at W54, and&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;7.9 (7.60),&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;239.27 (83.698), and 19.8 (13.99) at W102. For UC patients, the mean (SD) change from baseline for MMS, PMS, and SIBDQ, respectively, was&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;5.0 (2.23),&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;5.1 (2.01), and 21.5 (13.23) at W54, and&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;5.4 (1.79),&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;5.4 (1.59), and 23.0 (13.14) at W102. Scores for CDAI and SIBDQ in CD patients, and PMS and SIBDQ in UC patients, were well maintained throughout the maintenance and extension phases (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Figure S2</xref>). In patients who had their dose adjusted on or after W22, SES-CD and CDAI (CD study), MMS and PMS (UC study), and SIBDQ scores (CD and UC) taken immediately before dose adjustment had improved by W102, in both the CT-P13 SC and placebo groups, showing a benefit of treatment with CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in case of loss of response (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables S3</xref> and S<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">4</xref>). The proportion of patients in the CT-P13 SC and placebo groups of the CD and UC studies who achieved efficacy outcomes at W102 after undergoing dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg is shown in <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables S5</xref> and S<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">6</xref>.</p><p>In the placebo groups, when analyzed regardless of dose adjustment (dose-adjusted patients were not considered non-responders/non-remitters), the proportion of patients achieving efficacy endpoints at W102 (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S7</xref>) were similar to those seen in the CT-P13 SC groups (<xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figures 4</xref> and <xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>), indicating that disease control could be recaptured after dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg.</p><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5.</label><caption><p>Kaplan&#x02013;Meier plots of drug persistence (all-randomized population) in the CD study (A) and the UC study (B). Patients in the CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg group who received at least one administration of study drug after randomization are included in this figure. Time (weeks) to drug persistence is derived as the last study drug administration visit for patients with early discontinuation of study drug administration. Patients who completed study drug administration at W102 are considered censored at W102. Patients were considered to be receiving study drug regardless of dose adjustment. CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; SC, subcutaneous; UC, ulcerative colitis; W, week.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jjaf060_fig5" position="float"><alt-text>Drug persistence</alt-text></graphic></fig></sec><sec id="s15"><title>3.2 Pharmacokinetics</title><p>In the CT-P13 SC group, mean pre-dose serum concentrations of infliximab were well maintained during the maintenance and extension phases, ranging from 13.09 to 15.25 &#x003bc;g/mL for CD, and from 15.03 to 16.42 &#x003bc;g/mL for UC. For the placebo group, in which CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg treatment commenced at W56, mean (SD) pre-dose serum concentrations of infliximab gradually increased between W54 and W102 (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Figure S3</xref>).</p></sec><sec id="s16"><title>3.3. Biomarkers</title><p>Median concentrations of CRP and FC were generally low across both studies after study drug administration and were generally maintained for the CT-P13 SC group (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Figure S4</xref>). In both studies, the median concentration of CRP and FC in the placebo group decreased during the extension phase, following CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg initiation at W56.</p></sec><sec id="s17"><title>3.4. Drug persistence</title><p>In moderate-to-severe CD or UC, the majority of patients in the all-randomized population retained CT-P13 SC treatment at the end of the study (<xref rid="F5" ref-type="fig">Figure 5</xref>). In the CD study, among 148 patients who achieved clinical remission at W54, 93.2% (138/148) patients retained clinical remission at W102 (as-observed, regardless of dose adjustment; <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S8</xref>). In the UC study, among 116 patients who achieved clinical remission at W54, 87.1% (101/116) patients retained clinical remission at W102 (as-observed, regardless of dose adjustment; <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S8</xref>).</p></sec><sec id="s18"><title>3.5. Safety</title><p>A pooled summary of safety data for patients treated in the extension phase, combining TEAEs occurring during the maintenance and extension phases of the CD and UC studies is shown in <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>. The exposure-adjusted rate of patients experiencing &#x02265; 1 TEAE was similar between CT-P13 SC 120 and 240&#x000a0;mg and was higher with placebo. When only considering TEAEs related to the study drug, the exposure-adjusted rate of patients experiencing&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;1 events was similar between all the groups. The proportion and rate of patients having&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;1 TESAE was similar between CT-P13 SC 120 and 240&#x000a0;mg and to placebo treatment. No patients had TEAEs leading to study-drug discontinuation during placebo treatment (as the analysis was conducted in patients who continued into the extension phase and the analysis for placebo-treated patients only included TEAEs that occurred in the maintenance phase due to all patients receiving CT-P13 SC in the extension phase) while the rates during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120 and 240&#x000a0;mg were similar between groups. Rates of AESIs were similar for CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg, CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, and placebo except for localized ISRs and related infections, which were lower and higher, respectively, with placebo relative to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg and CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg. One patient (0.5%; 0.35 patients/100 person-year [PY]) experienced delayed hypersensitivity with CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg. There was one case (0.5%; 0.35 patients/100 PY) of malignancy that occurred in a CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg treated patient who received dose escalation following assignment to placebo treatment during the maintenance phase; this was deemed unrelated to treatment.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Frequency and exposure-adjusted incidence rate of TEAEs during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg, 240&#x000a0;mg, or placebo across the maintenance and extension phases, in patients treated in the extension phase (pooled analysis of safety populations treated in the extension phase).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" width="100%" border="1"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" colspan="7" rowspan="1">CD and UC</th></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">CT-P13 SC<break/>120 mg<break/>(<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;529, PY&#x02005;=&#x02005;728.76)</th><th align="left" colspan="3" rowspan="1">CT-P13 SC<break/>240 mg<break/>(<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;217, PY&#x02005;=&#x02005;282.23)</th><th align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">Placebo<break/>(<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;186, PY&#x02005;=&#x02005;113.89)</th></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Per 100 PY</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Per 100 PY</th><th align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Per 100 PY</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">368 (69.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.50</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">160 (73.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56.69</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">101 (54.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">88.68</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">116 (21.9)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.92</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">49 (22.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17.36</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">25 (13.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21.95</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TESAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45 (8.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.17</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17 (7.8)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.02</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">5 (2.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.39</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.14</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TEAE leading to study-drug discontinuation</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (1.9)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.37</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (3.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.83</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 (0.9)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.69</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.77</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of SIR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.65</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (2.8)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.13</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">3 (1.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.63</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.65</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (2.8)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.13</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">3 (1.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.63</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of delayed hypersensitivity</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of localized ISR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34 (6.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.67</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (3.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.83</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34 (6.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.67</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (3.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.83</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">183 (34.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25.11</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">88 (40.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.18</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">40 (21.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35.12</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27 (5.1)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.70</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (5.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.25</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">12 (6.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.54</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of serious infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">17 (3.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.33</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.77</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.14</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of malignancy</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="T1Fn1"><p>&#x0201c;<bold>CT-P13 SC 120 mg</bold>&#x0201d; comprises patients randomized at W10 to receive CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg during the maintenance phase and who continued to the extension phase (regardless of dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in either phase), and includes the data prior to any dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, AND patients who were randomized at W10 to receive placebo and were then switched to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg in the extension phase (and includes the data for these patients prior to any dose adjustment to CT-P13 240 SC mg).</p></fn><fn id="T1Fn2"><p>&#x0201c;<bold>CT-P13 SC 240 mg</bold>&#x0201d; comprises all patients with dose adjustment after W22, irrespective of whether the patient was randomized at W10 to receive placebo or CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg.</p></fn><fn id="T1Fn3"><p>&#x0201c;<bold>Placebo</bold>&#x0201d; comprises all patients randomized at W10 to receive placebo during the maintenance phase, then switched to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg upon entering the extension phase, or who underwent dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in the maintenance phase, and includes the data prior to switching to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or dose adjustment.</p></fn><fn id="T1Fn4"><p>The person-year for each patient is calculated as the (last visit date&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;date of first administration of each treatment&#x02005;+&#x02005;1)/365.25. For patients with any change in treatment due to dose adjustment, the duration of previous treatment is calculated as (date of first administration of later treatment&#x02005;&#x02212;&#x02005;date of first administration of each treatment)/365.25. Unless otherwise specified, events were considered unrelated to the study drug.</p></fn><fn id="T1Fn5"><p>CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; ISR, injection-site reaction; PY, person-year; SC, subcutaneous; SIR, systemic injection reaction; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event; TESAE, treatment-emergent serious adverse event; UC, ulcerative colitis; W, week.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>The most common TEAEs across the maintenance and extension phases are shown in <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S9</xref>. The most common TEAE during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg and CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg was COVID-19 (86 patients [16.3%, 11.80 patients/100 PY] and 43 patients [19.8%, 15.24 patients/100 PY], respectively).</p><p>A similar proportion of patients in each treatment group had at least one TEAE of grade&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;3 severity across the maintenance and extension phases (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S10</xref>). The most common grade&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;3 TEAEs during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg were neutropenia and blood creatine phosphokinase increased, both in 10 patients (1.9%, 1.37 patients/100 PY). The most common grade&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;3 TEAEs during treatment with CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg was neutropenia in 8 patients (3.7%, 2.83 patients/100 PY). One patient receiving CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg had grade 4 neutropenia that was considered related to study treatment.</p><p>The proportion of patients experiencing&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;1 treatment-emergent serious adverse event (TESAE) across the maintenance and extension phases was similar with CT-P13 SC 120 and 240&#x000a0;mg (8.5%, 6.17 patients/100 PY and 7.8%, 6.02 patients/100 PY, respectively). The most common TESAEs during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg were Crohn&#x02019;s disease (4 [0.8%] patients, 0.55 patients/100 PY) and COVID-19 pneumonia (3 [0.6%] patients, 0.41 patients/100 PY). The most common TESAEs during treatment with CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg were iron deficiency anemia (3 [1.4%] patients, 1.06 patients/100 PY) and COVID-19 pneumonia (2 [0.9%] patients, 0.71 patients/100 PY). One patient had grade 3 urinary tract infection during treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg which was considered related to treatment and one patient receiving placebo in the maintenance phase had grade 3 cellulitis which was considered to be related to study treatment (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S10</xref>).</p><p>Summaries of TEAEs by individual study phase are shown in <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref> (maintenance phase) and <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref> (extension phase). Overall TEAEs were generally similar across treatment, with no meaningful increases. However, one patient had a localized ISR with placebo in the maintenance phase increasing to 10 patients (5.4%) following the switch to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg in the extension phase.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2.</label><caption><p>Summary of TEAEs in the maintenance phase (pooled analysis of safety populations treated in extension phase).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" width="100%" border="1"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Patients, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">CD and UC</th></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CT-P13 SC<xref rid="T2Fn1" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>*</sup></xref> (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;440)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Placebo<xref rid="T2Fn2" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>&#x02020;</sup></xref> (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;186)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">305 (69.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">103 (55.4)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">95 (21.6)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">26 (14.0)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TESAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 (5.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 (2.7)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TEAE leading to study-drug discontinuation</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (0.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of SIR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (2.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (2.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of delayed hypersensitivity</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of localized ISR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22 (5.0)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22 (5.0)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">130 (29.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">40 (21.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (4.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12 (6.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;TEAE of serious infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of malignancy</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="T2Fn1"><p>
<sup>*</sup>Includes patients who underwent dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg.</p></fn><fn id="T2Fn2"><p>
<sup>&#x02020;</sup>Includes data prior to dose adjustment for patients who underwent dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg.</p></fn><fn id="T2Fn3"><p>CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; ISR, injection-site reaction; SC, subcutaneous; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event; TESAE, treatment-emergent serious adverse event; SIR, systemic injection reaction; UC, ulcerative colitis.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>Table 3.</label><caption><p>Summary of TEAEs in extension phase (pooled analysis of safety populations treated in extension phase).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" width="100%" border="1"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Patients, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic> (%)</th><th align="left" colspan="2" rowspan="1">CD and UC</th></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CT-P13 SC<xref rid="T3Fn1" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>*</sup></xref> (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;440)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Placebo<xref rid="T3Fn2" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>&#x02020;</sup></xref> (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x02005;=&#x02005;186)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">233 (53.0)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">108 (58.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">55 (12.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">28 (15.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TESAE</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">28 (6.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (4.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TEAE leading to study-drug discontinuation</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (3.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (1.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (1.6)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of SIR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (0.9)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (0.9)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of delayed hypersensitivity</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (0.2)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of localized ISR</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11 (2.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (5.4)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11 (2.5)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (5.4)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">122 (27.7)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">55 (29.6)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 (3.4)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (4.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;TEAE of serious infection</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 (2.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (1.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Related</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;1 TEAE of malignancy</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="T3Fn1"><p>
<sup>*</sup>Includes patients who underwent dose adjustment to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg.</p></fn><fn id="T3Fn2"><p>
<sup>&#x02020;</sup>Patients still receiving placebo at the end of the maintenance phase were switched to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg during the extension phase and includes patients regardless of who had dose adjusted to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in the maintenance or extension phase.</p></fn><fn id="T3Fn3"><p>CD, Crohn&#x02019;s disease; ISR, injection-site reaction; SC, subcutaneous; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event; TESAE, treatment-emergent serious adverse event; SIR, systemic injection reaction; UC, ulcerative colitis.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>The most common TEAEs occurring in&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;5% of patients in each study phase are shown in <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S11</xref> (maintenance phase) and <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S12</xref> (extension phase).</p><p>TEAEs grade&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;3 and TESAEs occurring in each study phase are shown in <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S13</xref> (maintenance phase) and <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S14</xref> (extension phase).</p><p>A summary of TEAEs for patients randomized to placebo at W10, both during placebo treatment in the maintenance phase, and after either switching to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg in the extension phase or dose-adjusting to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg in the maintenance phase, are shown in <xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S15</xref>. Treatment-emergent adverse events were generally similar during placebo treatment and when receiving CT-P13 SC after placebo, with the exception of localized ISRs which were higher with CT-P13 SC (5.95 patients/100 PY) compared with placebo (0.88 patients/100 PY).</p><p>There were no cases of CD- or UC-related surgery during CT-P13 SC treatment in the maintenance and extension phases.</p></sec><sec id="s19"><title>3.6. Immunogenicity</title><p>Positive anti-drug antibody (ADA) conversion rates in the CT-P13 SC group up to W54 and W102 were 64.2% and 73.1% in the CD study, and 66.2% and 76.5% in the UC study. The median ADA titers (based on dilution factors) of the CT-P13 SC group were maintained in both studies (189 at W54 and W102 in both the CD and UC studies).</p><p>Positive ADA conversion rates in the placebo group up to W54 (prior to dose adjustment only) and at W102 (regardless of dose adjustment) were 78.9% and 81.6% in the CD study, and 94.1% and 95.1% in the UC study&#x02014;the conversion rate did not increase. The median ADA titers (based on dilution factors) of the placebo group at W54 and W102 were 63 and 189, respectively, in the CD study, and 189 and 567, respectively, in the UC study, representing an increase of one dilution factor in each study.</p><p>In the CD study, the proportion of clinical remitters at W102 was comparable between ADA-positive and ADA-negative patients, while the proportion of endoscopic responders was lower in ADA-positive patients (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S16</xref>). There were no specific trends in the proportion of clinical remitters at W102 concerning quartiles of ADA titer, while the proportion of endoscopic remitters was lower in higher quartiles of ADA titer; however, the number of patients in each quartile was limited (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S16</xref>).</p><p>In the UC study, the proportion of clinical remitters at W102 was comparable by ADA status (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S17</xref>). The proportion of patients who achieved clinical remission was lower in the highest ADA quartile than in the lower quartiles; however, the number of patients in each quartile was limited (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S17</xref>).</p><p>There was no apparent impact of ADA status or ADA titer on safety in the maintenance and extension phases (<xref rid="sup1" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Table S18</xref>).</p></sec></sec><sec id="s20"><title>4. Discussion</title><p>Results from these 2 phase 3 open-label extension phases build upon the W54 findings from the maintenance phase (up to W54) of the study,<sup><xref rid="CIT0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> and show long-term efficacy of CT-P13 SC as a maintenance treatment for moderate-to-severe CD and UC.</p><p>Approximately 80% of patients in the all-randomized CT-P13 SC and placebo groups in each study were treated in the extension phase, representing a high retention rate after 1 year of follow-up. Efficacy in patients treated in the extension phase was well maintained in the CT-P13 SC group from W54 to W102 using both NRI and as-observed approaches. This aligns with real-world data from prospective and retrospective studies supporting the efficacy and safety of CT-P13 IV as long-term maintenance therapy for CD and UC.<sup><xref rid="CIT0023" ref-type="bibr">23&#x02013;26</xref></sup> For example, Kazama and colleagues (2023) reported a 2-year clinical remission rate of 72.0% and 33.3% in patients with CD and UC, respectively, who switched from reference infliximab to an infliximab biosimilar. In CD patients, depending on their symptoms, the dose of infliximab biosimilar was increased from IV 5&#x000a0;mg/kg to 10&#x000a0;mg/kg and the treatment interval was shortened to 4 weeks. Equivalent analyses from the current study observed a similar (CD: 79.2%, <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3C</xref>) or higher (UC: 60.1%, <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4B</xref>) clinical remission rate. Moreover, Tursi and colleagues (2019) reported a 2-year clinical remission rate of 75.6% and 57.3% in patients receiving infliximab biosimilar (CT-P13; 5&#x000a0;mg/kg) with CD and UC, respectively. Dose escalation to 10&#x000a0;mg/kg and/or every 4 instead of 8 weeks was permitted, where needed. Equivalent analyses from the current study observed a similar clinical remission rate (CD: 79.2%, <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3C</xref>; UC: 55.7%, <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4C</xref>). In addition, Kim and colleagues (2019) reported a 2-year clinical remission rate of 75.3% and 59.1% in anti-TNF-na&#x000ef;ve patients receiving infliximab biosimilar (CT-P13; 5&#x000a0;mg/kg) with CD and UC (as-observed analyses). Equivalent analyses from the current study observed comparable clinical remission rates (CD: 70.5%, <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Figure 3B</xref>; UC: 60.1%, <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4B</xref>). However, a cautious approach to comparison should be taken due to the different definitions of clinical remission used in the respective studies. Finally, as these describe data for IV administration, further study of long-term SC administration is needed.</p><p>Reports of the frequency of loss of response in studies in CD and UC in the literature vary, but loss of response in the CT-P13 SC group (determined by initiation of dose adjustment) was generally in line with those reported with infliximab in the literature.<sup><xref rid="CIT0027" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>,<xref rid="CIT0028" ref-type="bibr">28</xref></sup> Preliminary analyses suggest that baseline characteristics such as weight and disease severity had no effect on the requirement for dose adjustment, whereas the response to induction treatment at W10, in terms of PK and remission, may have influenced the later need for dose adjustment; these initial findings warrant further investigation. Comparing efficacy endpoint values prior to dose adjustment (ie, at the point of loss of response) with those at W102 showed an improvement in disease control following dose adjustment. Furthermore, efficacy outcomes were maintained regardless of dose adjustment, indicating that dose adjustment from CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg to 240&#x000a0;mg may be effective in restoring efficacy in long-term treatment.</p><p>In the placebo group, when dose-adjusted patients were not automatically considered non-responders/non-remitters, efficacy results at W102 approached those seen in patients who received continuous treatment with CT-P13 SC throughout the study. Reintroducing CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg or escalating in patients with loss of response from 120 to 240&#x000a0;mg or from placebo to CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg leads to a recapture of response in many patients. Reattainment of disease control in the placebo group offers a potential explanation for the overall high retention rate in this group at the end of the study extension phase. These findings offer benefits in clinical practice, presenting clinicians with the opportunity to reintroduce or escalate the dose of CT-P13 SC in patients who have previously tolerated it well.</p><p>Pharmacokinetic and biomarker parameters were well maintained up to W102 in the CT-P13 SC group. Serum concentrations of CT-P13 increased and biomarkers decreased during the extension phase in patients switching from placebo to CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg, with pre-dose serum levels of CT-P13 gradually increasing after receiving CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg from W56.</p><p>In this pooled analysis of patient safety data in the CT-P13 SC and placebo groups treated in the extension phase of the CD and UC studies, CT-P13 SC continued to be well tolerated up to 2 years of treatment, with comparable safety results observed during the maintenance and extension phases, with the exposure-adjusted incidence of TEAEs generally similar across treatment arms. Switching from placebo to CT-P13 SC group did not markedly increase the incidence of TEAEs. The most common TEAEs were as expected based on the known safety profile of infliximab,<sup><xref rid="CIT0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref></sup> with no new safety findings identified.</p><p>For those who maintained CT-P13 SC treatment into the extension phase, the similar safety profile throughout the study, combined with a low rate of grade&#x02005;&#x02265;&#x02005;3 TEAEs (1.1%) and no TESAEs considered related to study drug during the extension phase, support an absence of late safety issues with CT-P13 SC treatment. For those in the placebo group, the similarity of the overall safety profile during placebo treatment and after receiving CT-P13 SC, with only an elevation in localized ISRs noted with CT-P13 SC treatment following placebo, supports the suitability of restarting CT-P13 SC treatment after a treatment break. The low rate of TEAEs leading to study-drug discontinuation during the extension phase further supports the tolerability of CT-P13 SC for longer-term maintenance treatment.</p><p>Dose adjustment was well tolerated, with a similar rate of TEAEs reported in those who received CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg throughout the study and those who received an adjusted dose of CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg. Across the whole study period, ISRs were more common with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg compared with CT-P13 SC 240&#x000a0;mg, which is likely to reflect a higher incidence of ISRs during the earlier phase of treatment with CT-P13 SC as the occurrence of localized ISRs with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg treatment was observed to decline in the extension phase versus the maintenance phase.</p><p>An absence of cases of CD- or UC-related surgery during the maintenance and extension phases supports a protective effect of CT-P13 against the need for resection in patients with IBD, which has been observed for infliximab both in the clinical trial setting and, to a lesser degree, in observational studies.<sup><xref rid="CIT0029" ref-type="bibr">29</xref></sup></p><p>Anti-drug antibodies to biologics can modify their PK properties and impact efficacy.<sup><xref rid="CIT0030" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>,<xref rid="CIT0031" ref-type="bibr">31</xref></sup> Immunogenicity results up to W54 in the CT-P13 SC group (ADA-positive: 64.2% in the CD study and 66.2% in the UC study) were similar to those previously reported after 1 year of treatment with CT-P13 SC 120&#x000a0;mg (ADA-positive: 69.7%).<sup><xref rid="CIT0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> In the current study, the ADA conversion rate increased up to W102 compared to the rate apparent up to W54, but the ADA titers were comparable, and the mean pre-dose serum concentrations remained close to steady-state levels through W102. With low ADA titers and high/stable trough levels of CT-P13, there was no impact on efficacy despite the relatively higher ADA conversion rate.</p><p>The study period exceeded the minimum 12-month treatment period recommended by FDA guidance for demonstrating response durability,<sup><xref rid="CIT0032" ref-type="bibr">32</xref></sup> representing a strength of the current study. This extended study period should provide more comprehensive information regarding the long-term efficacy and safety of this drug, as well as better insights into response durability and, ultimately, the long-term use of CT-P13 SC in the management of these chronic diseases. The efficacy endpoints assessed in the extension phase were also based on FDA industry guidance.<sup><xref rid="CIT0032" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>,<xref rid="CIT0033" ref-type="bibr">33</xref></sup></p><p>The generalizability of the findings is limited by the trial setting, in which the patient population met set criteria, and protocolized interventions and set follow-up visits and measurements took place, which may not reflect real-world settings. As all patients received CT-P13 SC during the extension phase, there was no in-study long-term control arm. However, long-term IV outcomes are well known and the results, including efficacy and safety outcomes, were similar to those consequently expected. Analyses were conducted only for those patients who were considered likely to benefit from further treatment and continued into the extension phase, and therefore may not be representative of the expected outcomes of 2 years of treatment for all patients. Nonetheless, there was a high retention rate into the extension phase, with ~80% of patients in the all-randomized population continuing on study, and thus these outcomes are fairly representative of overall outcomes across 2 years of treatment.</p><p>In conclusion, the study results support the use of CT-P13 SC as an effective and well-tolerated long-term maintenance treatment for both CD and UC. The study also provides early evidence of the benefits of dose adjustment to regain disease control following loss of response.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Material</title><supplementary-material id="sup1" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>jjaf060_suppl_Supplementary_Tables_S1-S18_Figures_S1-S4</label><media xlink:href="jjaf060_suppl_supplementary_tables_s1-s18_figures_s1-s4.docx"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><ack id="a1"><title>Acknowledgments</title><p>We thank all patients and investigators involved in the study. Medical writing support, including the development of a draft outline and subsequent drafts in consultation with the authors, collating author comments, copyediting, fact-checking, and referencing, was provided by Julianna Solomons, PhD, CMPP, at Aspire Scientific Limited (Bollington, UK), funded by Celltrion, Inc. (Incheon, Republic of Korea).</p></ack><sec id="s22"><title>Author Contributions</title><p>J.-F.C., W.J.S., S.Sc., S.D., S.J.L., S.K., Y.B., Su.L., Se.L., Ju.L., G.P., B.E.S., and S.B.H. contributed to the study design. M.K&#x00142;., J.K., R.K., M.G., A.S., P.S., S.Sr., E.V., A.L., M.H., A.G., M.O., V.B., M.Ko., D.S., and R.S. contributed to data acquisition. J.-F.C., W.J.S., S.Sc., S.D., S.J.L., S.K., Y.B., Su.L., Se.L., J.H.L., J.M.K., G.P., Ji.L., Ju.L., J.Y.R., B.E.S., and S.B.H. interpreted the data. All authors reviewed and critically revised the manuscript, approved the final draft, and are accountable for accuracy and integrity.</p></sec><sec id="s23"><title>Funding</title><p>This work was supported by Celltrion, Inc. (Incheon, Republic of Korea). The study sponsor played a role in the study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, and preparation of the manuscript.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="s24"><title>Conflicts of interest</title><p>J.-.F.C. reports receiving research grants from AbbVie, Bristol Myers Squibb, Janssen Pharmaceuticals, Prometheus, and Takeda; receiving payment for lectures from AbbVie and Takeda; receiving consulting fees from AbbVie, Allergan, Amgen, AnaptysBio, Arena Pharmaceuticals, Astellas, Boehringer Ingelheim, Bristol Myers Squibb, Celgene, Celltrion, Eli Lilly, Envision Pharma Group, Ferring Pharmaceuticals, Galmed Research, Genentech (Roche), GlaxoSmithKline, Immunic, Iterative Scopes, Janssen Pharmaceuticals, Kaleido Biosciences, Landos Biopharma, Merck, Microba Life Sciences, Novartis, Otsuka Pharmaceutical, Pfizer, Protagonist Therapeutics, Sanofi, Sun Pharma, Takeda, TiGenix, and Vifor; and holding stock options in Intestinal Biotech Development.</p><p>W.J.S. reports receiving research grants from AbbVie, Abivax, Arena Pharmaceuticals, Boehringer Ingelheim, Celgene, Eli Lilly, Genentech (Roche), Gilead Sciences, GlaxoSmithKline, Janssen Pharmaceuticals, Pfizer, Prometheus Laboratories, Seres Therapeutics, Shire Pharmaceuticals, Takeda, and Theravance Biopharma; receiving consulting fees from AbbVie, Abivax, Admirx (now Q32 Bio), Alfasigma, Alimentiv, Alivio Therapeutics, Allakos, Amgen, Arena Pharmaceuticals, AstraZeneca, Atlantic Pharmaceuticals, Bausch Health (Salix), BeiGene, Bellatrix Pharmaceuticals, Biora (Progenity), Boehringer Ingelheim, Boston Pharmaceuticals, Bristol Myers Squibb, Celgene, Celltrion, ClostraBio, Codexis, Eli Lilly, Equillium, Forbion, Galapagos, Genentech (Roche), Gilead Sciences, GlaxoSmithKline, Gossamer Bio, Immunic (Vital Therapies), Index Pharmaceuticals, Inotrem, Intact Therapeutics, Iota B